# What happens when a few firms dominate?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What changes when each firm must think about its rivals? Why do we need game theory to answer it?"
next: {"title":"Why do rivals fail to cooperate?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/38-why-do-rivals-fail-to-cooperate.cast"}

## Price hunters
use: pie_chart
with:
    labels:
      - NorgesGruppen
      - Coop + Rema
      - Others
    values:
      - 43.5
      - 51.5
      - 5
    percent: true
    start_angle: -60
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 150
      w: 540
      h: 480
sources:
    - id: kt2024
      title: Coop, Norgesgruppen and Rema fined 4.9 billion NOK
      authors: Norwegian Competition Authority (Konkurransetilsynet)
      year: 2024
      finding: On 21 August 2024 the three chains, which together account for 95% of turnover in the Norwegian groceries market, were fined NOK 4.9 billion for exchanging price information through 'price hunters' with hand scanners in each other's stores; by the end they received rivals' prices several times a day. Upheld in full by the Competition Appeals Tribunal on 21 August 2025.
      url: https://konkurransetilsynet.no/coop-norgesgruppen-and-rema-fined-4-9-billion-nok/?lang=en
    - id: kt2025
      title: Dagligvarerapport 2024/25
      authors: Konkurransetilsynet
      year: 2025
      finding: NorgesGruppen had 43.5% of national grocery turnover in 2024; the three chains together over 95%.
      url: https://konkurransetilsynet.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Konkurransetilsynets-Dagligvarerapport-2024-25.pdf

    text big95 "95%" #b5482e cites ["kt2024","kt2025"] x 790 y 540 font_size 96 hidden true
    text names "three chains" x 790 y 465 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon scanner size 80 x 690 y 350 or ["barcode","magnifying glass"] icon_look picture of "barcode scanner" hidden true
    text hunt "price hunters" x 840 y 350 font_size 30 hidden true
    text fine "4.9 bn kroner" #b5482e cites ["kt2024"] x 790 y 240 font_size 52 hidden true
    text taker "Price taker: no rival to watch" x 790 y 350 font_size 26 hidden true
    text monop "Monopolist: no rival at all" x 790 y 270 font_size 26 hidden true
    text hold "rivals hold → shoppers switch" #2f6b8f x 790 y 350 font_size 26 hidden true
    text match "rivals match → nobody switches" #b5482e x 790 y 280 font_size 26 hidden true
    text loop "my demand depends on yours" #8a5fa8 x 790 y 195 font_size 30 hidden true
    text fine_sub "fine, 2024" #b5482e x 790 y 190 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "What happens when a few firms dominate?"

What changes when a market has only a few sellers, each watching the others? Norway's groceries are a good place to look.
    draw slice_1 slice_2 slice_3

B: Three chains, NorgesGruppen,  (@draw names@)Coop and Rema, sell ninety-five percent of  (@draw big95@)the country's groceries.

And for years they let each other (@draw scanner hunt@)'s staff walk the aisles with hand scanners, recording prices. Price hunters.

B: In the end each chain got its rivals' prices several times a day. In 2024 the competition  (@draw fine fine_sub@)authority fined the three 4.9 billion kroner for that exchange of prices.

Why would a firm care that much about a rival's price list?
    highlight scanner effect circle

    erase scanner hunt fine fine_sub

A small fir (@draw taker@)m in a competitive market wouldn't. It takes the marke (@highlight taker effect underline@)t price, and no rival notices what it does.

B: And a m (@draw monop@)onopolist has no rival to watch. It only faces its buyers.

    fade taker monop to 0.25

    erase taker monop

    quiz "Rema cuts its milk price by 10%. What happens to its milk sales?" intro "So, a guess." judge false voice b
        right: Any of those can happen.
        * They jump
        * They barely move
        * Can't say yet

If Coop and NorgesGrup (@draw hold@)pen hold their prices, shoppers stream over to Rema.

If they match the  (@draw match@)cut within hours, almost nobody switches.

B: Which is why they paid people to watch. Rema's sales depend on Coop's price, and Coop's sales on Rema's.
    highlight slice_1 slice_2 effect pulse

    erase hold match

That's what changes with a few firms: no firm knows i (@draw loop@)ts own demand until it knows what the others will do.

It matters far beyond groceries, for airlines, banks, phone networks, and for anyone who must judge a merger.
    highlight loop effect underline

    pause 0.4

## Two demand curves
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,110]}
vars:
    p:
      value: 60
      min: 50
      max: 70
      step: 1
    Q:
      expr: min(4*(72.5 - p), (185 - p)/2.5)
    pct:
      expr: (Q - 50)*2
    mc:
      value: 35
      min: 10
      max: 60

    axes axes x_label "Rema's milk sales (Q)" y_label "Rema's price (P)" cross corner ticks.x false ticks.y false hidden true
    dot today #3d3833 at.x 50 at.y 60 hidden true
    label label_today "today" right attach_to today hidden true
    curve hold #2f6b8f expr "72.5 - 0.25*x" x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label label_hold "rivals hold" right #2f6b8f attach_to hold hidden true
    curve match #b5482e expr "185 - 2.5*x" x_from 30 x_to 74 hidden true
    label label_match "rivals match" right #b5482e attach_to match hidden true
    curve kink_up #3d3833 expr "72.5 - 0.25*x" x_from 0 x_to 50 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    curve kink_down #3d3833 expr "185 - 2.5*x" x_from 50 x_to 74 style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    text kink_name "Sweezy's kink, 1939" at.data [72,95] font_size 32 hidden true
    text rise_cue "rise: alone" #2f6b8f at.data [14,56] font_size 28 hidden true
    text cut_cue "cut: all follow" #b5482e at.data [80,40] font_size 28 hidden true
    dot pt #8a5fa8 at.x 50 at.y 60 bind {"at.x":"Q","at.y":"p"} hidden true
    text readout "price {p:0} → sales change {pct:0}%" #8a5fa8 at.data [70,95] font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    curve mr_up dashed #2f6b8f expr "72.5 - 0.5*x" x_from 0 x_to 50 hidden true
    text label_mr "MR" #2f6b8f at.data [30,48] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve gap_mr #f2c14e x_expr "50" y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 47.5 style.stroke_width 10 hidden true
    text label_gap "gap, MR < 0 below" at.data [34,20] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve mcline #87a878 expr mc x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label label_mc "MC" right #5d7d4f attach_to mcline hidden true
    text mr_neg "steep piece: MR < 0" #b5482e at.data [78,82] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Two demand curves"

To see why, draw Rema's demand for milk: at each price, how much it sells. Here is today, with illustrative numbers.
    draw axes today label_today

B: If the rivals keep thei (@draw hold label_hold@)r prices whatever Rema does, its demand is flat. Cut a little and you steal lots of shoppe (@point at.data [80,52.5] gesture tap@)rs; raise a little and you lose them.

And if the r (@draw match label_match@)ivals match every move, it's steep. A cut takes the whole (@point at.data [62,30] gesture tap@) market down with it, and nobody changes shop.

B: So which one is Rema's demand curve?
    highlight hold match

Neither, until you guess what the rivals will do. In 1939 the (@draw kink_name@) economist Paul Sweezy made a guess: rivals match a cut, but ignore a rise.
    point at.ref today gesture circle

B: Raise  (@draw rise_cue@)your price and you're alone; cut (@draw cut_cue@) it and everyone follows.

    fade hold match label_hold label_match to 0.25

B: Then Re (@draw kink_up kink_down@)ma's demand is flat above today's price and steep below it. A kink.

    erase rise_cue cut_cue

So from the kink, no move pays. A rise loses many customers; a cut wins hardly any and lowers the price on every litre.
    highlight today effect pulse

    erase kink_name

    draw pt readout

B: Try it: slide Rema's price up and down and watch its sales under Sweezy's guess.
    explore params ["p"]

    animate p 66 duration 1.5

    animate p 54 duration 2

The price stays stuck at the kink. It's an answer, but it came straight out of one assumption about how rivals react.
    animate p 60 duration 1.2

    erase pt readout

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want to see why even a change in costs needn't move the price?

@math

    erase mathq

Each straight piece has its  (@draw mr_up label_mr@)own marginal revenue, MR, falling twice as fast as its demand line. On the st (@draw mr_neg@)eep piece it is already below zero.

At the kink, MR jumps (@draw gap_mr label_gap@) from this line down below zero, and leaves a gap.

If marginal  (@draw mcline label_mc@)cost, MC, crosses anywhere inside that gap, MR equals MC at the same quantity, and the price stays put.

    animate mc 25 duration 1.5

    animate mc 42 duration 1.5

    erase mr_up label_mr gap_mr label_gap mcline label_mc mr_neg

@test

    erase mathq

    highlight kink_up kink_down duration 1.5

## From guesses to a game
use: payoff_matrix
with:
    row_player: Mia's bakery
    col_player: Rival
    row_strategies:
      - Big batch
      - Small batch
    col_strategies:
      - Big batch
      - Small batch
    payoffs:
      - [[2, 2], [5, 3]]
      - [[3, 5], [4, 4]]
    highlight_equilibrium: true
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 150
      w: 700
      h: 520
sources:
    - id: stigler1947
      title: The Kinky Oligopoly Demand Curve and Rigid Prices
      authors: George J. Stigler
      year: 1947
      finding: Journal of Political Economy 55(5). Rivals often did follow price increases (e.g. in steel), and monopoly prices, which face no kink, changed even less often than oligopoly prices — evidence against the kink as the cause of price rigidity.
      url: https://econpapers.repec.org/RePEc:ucp:jpolec:v:55:y:1947:p:432
    - id: sweezy1939
      title: Demand Under Conditions of Oligopoly
      authors: Paul M. Sweezy
      year: 1939
      finding: 'Journal of Political Economy 47(4). The kinked demand curve: rivals follow a price cut but not a rise, so price tends to stick.'
    - id: nash1950
      title: Equilibrium Points in N-Person Games
      authors: John F. Nash
      year: 1950
      finding: 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 36(1). Defines an equilibrium point: each player''s strategy is a best reply to the others''; proves one exists (in mixed strategies) in every finite game.'

    path kaxes #8f887c points [[150,580],[150,260],[580,260]] hidden true
    path kline #3d3833 points [[170,500],[360,450],[540,290]] style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    text kname "Sweezy 1939" cites ["sweezy1939"] x 420 y 540 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark kstrike around kline kind cross hidden true
    text stig "Stigler 1947: rises were followed" #b5482e cites ["stigler1947"] x 365 y 205 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cournot "Cournot 1838: rivals keep Q" #2f6b8f x 360 y 500 font_size 34 hidden true
    text bertrand "Bertrand 1883: rivals keep P" #b5482e x 360 y 420 font_size 34 hidden true
    text differ "same market, different answers" x 360 y 320 font_size 32 hidden true
    text profits "profit, $100s (illustrative)" #8f887c x 860 y 615 font_size 22 hidden true
    portrait nash cites ["nash1950"] x 850 y 430 width 200 of "John Forbes Nash Jr." cameo true hidden true
    text two "two equilibria" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text consensus "game theory: consensus" #3d6b3a x 850 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    text contested "which game: contested" #b5482e x 850 y 260 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "From guesses to a game"

B: The kink stands on one guess about rivals. In 1947 George Stigler went looking for it in the data.
    draw kaxes kline kname

He found (@draw stig@) rivals did follow price rises, in steel for one. And monopolists, with no rivals to kink anything, ch (@draw kstrike@)anged prices even less often than oligopolists.

B: So the kink explains a stickiness that isn't specially there, and never says how the price got to the kink in the first place.
    highlight kline effect pulse

    erase kaxes kline kname kstrike stig

The trouble is older than Sweezy. Cournot i (@draw cournot@)n 1838 assumed rivals keep their quantities; Bertra (@draw bertrand@)nd in 1883, that they keep their prices. Same marke (@draw differ@)t, very different answers.

B: Every guess about the rival gives a new theory. What we need is a rule for which guesses can be right.
    fade cournot bertrand to 0.3

    erase cournot bertrand differ

That rule comes from game theory: write down who plays, what each can do, and what each earns.
    draw grid row_player_label col_player_label row_label_0 row_label_1 col_label_0 col_label_1

The grocers' puzzle in miniature: Mia's b (@draw cell_0_0 cell_0_1 cell_1_0 cell_1_1 profits@)akery and a new rival, each baking a big or a small Saturday batch, with illustrative profits.

B: If the rival bakes big, Mia does best small: three beats two, so her three is underlined.
    highlight cell_1_0 effect box

B: If the rival bakes small, Mia does best big. Five beats four. Her best move flips with his.
    highlight cell_0_1 effect box

And his flips with hers. John Nash's idea, from 195 (@draw nash@)0: an equilibrium is a cell where each is already making the best rep (@draw nash_0_1 nash_1_0 parallel true@)ly to the other, so nobody's guess turns out wrong.

    erase nash

B: Here there (@draw two@) are two. Game theory tells you which outcomes can last, not always which one you get.

    erase two

How sure are we? That rivalry must be analysed a (@draw consensus@)s a game is consensus: it is the backbone of modern industrial economics.

B: Which game a real market plays, prices or quantities, once or every day, is c (@draw contested@)ontested. And that choice decides almost everything.

    erase consensus contested

    quiz "A town has 300 cafés. One cuts its coffee price. Must it think about how the others react?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Hardly. One café in three hundred is too small for rivals to notice, so it can take the market price as given.
        wrong: Would any of the other 299 even notice one café's price? Hardly: it can take the market price as given.
        * Yes, they'll match at once
        + Hardly: no one will notice
        * Only if it's a monopolist

    quiz "Two ferry companies share one route. One cuts its fare; the other matches the next day. What did the first one gain?" voice b
        right: Few new passengers, and a lower fare on every ticket. When the rival matches, nobody has a reason to switch ferry.
        wrong: If both fares fall, why would a passenger switch ferry? So: few new passengers, and a lower fare on every ticket.
        * Many new passengers
        + Few new passengers, a lower fare on every ticket
        * Nothing changes at all

    quiz "Two airlines each pick a fare for one route. Which pair of fares is a Nash equilibrium?"
        right: A pair where each fare is the best reply to the other's: neither airline gains by changing its fare alone.
        wrong: Ask whether either airline would change its fare on its own. The answer: a pair where each fare is the best reply to the other's.
        * The pair that earns them most together
        + A pair where each fare is the best reply to the other's
        * The pair a regulator sets

So what changes when a few firms dominate? Batches or milk prices, each firm's best move depends on the others' moves, and theirs on its own.
    highlight nash_0_1 nash_1_0

That loop is why the price hunters were in the aisles, and why economists needed game theory to close it.
    highlight grid

    pause 0.4

## Where next
