    '|a||The trouble is older than Sweezy. Cournot in 1838 assumed rivals keep their quantities; Bertrand in 1883, that they keep their prices. Same market, very different answers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:o5atc4'
    '|b||Every guess about the rival gives a new theory. What we need is a rule for which guesses can be right.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6400
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1yj5meu'
    '|a||That rule comes from game theory: write down who plays, what each can do, and what each earns.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:29tq7f'
    '|a||The grocers'' puzzle in miniature: Mia''s bakery and a new rival, each baking a big or a small Saturday batch, with illustrative profits.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9200
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1jaybki'
    '|b||If the rival bakes big, Mia does best small: three beats two, so her three is underlined.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:azsysm'
    '|b||If the rival bakes small, Mia does best big. Five beats four. Her best move flips with his.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7240
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:uw2ujb'
    '|a||And his flips with hers. John Nash''s idea, from 1950: an equilibrium is a cell where each is already making the best reply to the other, so nobody''s guess turns out wrong.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11640
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1nmo5nw'
    '|b||Here there are two. Game theory tells you which outcomes can last, not always which one you get.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:64ny0p'
    '|a||How sure are we? That rivalry must be analysed as a game is consensus: it is the backbone of modern industrial economics.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:f49nuu'
    '|b||Which game a real market plays, prices or quantities, once or every day, is contested. And that choice decides almost everything.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1ng2htt'
    '|a||Three new cases. A town has 300 cafés. One cuts its coffee price. Must it think about how the others react?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:wr7lti'
    '|a||Hardly. One café in three hundred is too small for rivals to notice, so it can take the market price as given.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7320
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Two ferry companies share one route. One cuts its fare; the other matches the next day. What did the first one gain?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Few new passengers, and a lower fare on every ticket. When the rival matches, nobody has a reason to switch ferry.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7480
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Two airlines each pick a fare for one route. Which pair of fares is a Nash equilibrium?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6680
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||A pair where each fare is the best reply to the other''s: neither airline gains by changing its fare alone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||So what changes when a few firms dominate? Batches or milk prices, each firm''s best move depends on the others'' moves, and theirs on its own.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11080
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||That loop is why the price hunters were in the aisles, and why economists needed game theory to close it.':
